# ── Build artifacts ────────
# Unanchored on purpose. These were root-anchored (`/target/`, `fuzz/target/`)
# for the Rust layout; `cyrius port` moved the whole Rust tree under `rust-old/`
# and every one of those rules silently stopped matching — `rust-old/target/`
# alone is 7.6 GB and was staged by `git add -A` until this was fixed. Matching
# at any depth survives the next move too.
target/
build/

# `lib/` (cyrius deps-vendored stdlib) and `dist/` (the published bundle plus
# its .deps sidecar) are deliberately TRACKED, matching prakash and mabda —
# `cyrius deps` validates a consumer's `[deps] stdlib` against the sidecar, so
# it is part of the published contract. Do not add them here.

# Cyrius toolchain tarball (fetched during CI)
cyrius-*.tar.gz

# ── rust-old/ ────────
# The frozen Rust 1.0.1 archive is kept 1–3 releases as the translation
# reference, then deleted with a CHANGELOG entry naming the
# `git checkout 1.0.1 -- rust-old/` recovery incantation. Its source, manifests,
# Cargo.lock, and supply-chain audits are all TRACKED — Cargo.lock in particular
# records the exact audited dependency set that 1.0.1 shipped against, which is
# the point of keeping the archive. Only its build output is ignored, by the
# `target/` rule above.
#
# Nothing else here: the Rust-era coverage, criterion, proptest and cargo-vet
# cache rules were dropped when the port ran. None of those tools run against a
# Cyrius tree, and their paths no longer exist.

# ── Editor / IDE ────────
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml

# ── OS files ────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Debugging ────────
*.pdb
core
core.*

# ── Claude Code ────────
.claude/

# ── Environment / secrets ────────
.env
.env.*
